+(co-)evaluation maps exist over $R$. This proof is an identity calculation
+in $R$, not an appeal to validity at its complex points; applying any
+coefficient homomorphism preserves it.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{Flatness over the dual numbers}
+Base change along $R\to A$ gives $\rho_A$ and all the split maps.
+In particular,
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:split-fusion-A}
+ \rho_A^2\cong\bigoplus_{g\in G}\alpha_g\oplus\rho_A^{\oplus N},
+ \qquad\alpha_g\rho_A=\rho_A,\qquad\rho_A\alpha_g\cong\rho_A.
+\end{equation}
+We do not infer flatness from the mere existence of algebraic formulas.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Flat split skeleton]\label{lem:flat-skeleton}
+The additive tensor category generated by $\alpha_g$ and $\rho_A$ has
+free finite-rank Hom modules with the same ranks as its complex reduction.
+A choice of the split skeleton and fusion bases lifting the special-fiber
+choices presents it as a first-order deformation of the fusion category
+constructed in \cite[Section~5.4]{RW2}.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+The complex category has scalar endomorphisms of the basic simple labels
+$\alpha_g,\rho$, and zero morphisms between distinct such labels, by the
+Hom calculations in \cite[Section~5.4]{RW2}. In
+$L_{2N}(A)=A\otimes_\C L_{2N}$ write an intertwiner as
+$z=z_0+\eps z_1$. Between distinct basic labels, reduction gives $z_0=0$;
+the coefficient of $\eps$ in the intertwining equation then makes $z_1$
+an intertwiner between the distinct reduced labels, so $z_1=0$.
+For an endomorphism of a basic label, $z_0$ is scalar. Subtracting that
+scalar identity, which is itself an exact intertwiner over $A$, reduces
+to the same argument and makes $z_1$ scalar. Hence
+\[
+ \operatorname{End}_A(\alpha_g)=A,\quad
+ \operatorname{End}_A(\rho_A)=A,\quad
+ \Hom_A(S,T)=0\quad(S,T\text{ distinct basic labels}).
+\]
+The explicit decompositions \eqref{eq:split-fusion-A} now reduce every
+tensor word to a direct sum of those labels. Hom modules are therefore
+matrices over $A$ of the required sizes. Idempotent completion adds no
+new types: finite projective modules over the local ring $A$ are free.
+The split maps identify the tensor bifunctor with its fixed fusion-rule
+model; the transported associator reduces to that of the complex category.
+The snake identities give rigidity. Thus this is a flat first-order tensor
+deformation, without claiming semisimplicity over the nonfield $A$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{Intrinsic reconstruction and monoidal gauge invariance}
+We next extend the normalization in \cite[Section~5.5]{RW2}. It is
+important to retain the associator: our endomorphism realization is strict,
+but the skeleton used for deformation theory need not be.
+Write $\mathfrak a_{X,Y,Z}:(X\otimes Y)\otimes Z\to X\otimes(Y\otimes Z)$.
+Identity morphisms and unit identifications are suppressed where unambiguous.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Normalized scalar reconstruction]\label{lem:intrinsic-reconstruction}
+For the category of Lemma~\ref{lem:flat-skeleton}, and for any of its
+$A$-linearly strongly monoidally equivalent labelled models, the scalars
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:intrinsic-coordinate}
+ \mathcal I_g1_\rho=
+ T_0'(T_0'\otimes1)\mathfrak a_{\rho,\rho,\rho}^{-1}
+                  (1\otimes T_g)T_0
+\end{equation}
+are intrinsically defined by the normalization below. They are invariant
+under such equivalences and, in the Leavitt realization, satisfy
 \begin{equation}\label{eq:recon}
- \frac{E_A(g)}s\,1_\rho
- =T'_0(T'_0\otimes1_\rho)(1_\rho\otimes T_g)T_0.
+ \mathcal I_g=E_A(g)/s.
 \end{equation}
-All tensor associativity identifications are understood in this expression.
-The two basis factors and two dual-basis factors cancel the common rescaling.
-Thus the right side is a scalar invariant of the labelled monoidal category,
-not a coordinate depending on a moving basis.
-Under the equivalence to the constant deformation it is therefore
-$E(g)/s$. Relabelling by $\Aut(G)$ cannot create a first-order motion of labels;
-the equivalence is chosen to have fixed reduction. Equation~\eqref{eq:recon}
-gives $E_A(g)=E(g)$, hence $h(g)=0$ for all $g$.
-
-\subsection{Finishing the scheme argument}
-Under Hypothesis~\ref{hyp:realization}, every nonexceptional complex point
-has $T_EX_{G,q}=0$.
-At the order-three exception, the Jacobian of the residuals $P_{xy}$ in
-variables $(E(0),E(1),E(2),C)$ has a nonzero $4\times4$ minor
-\[
- -\frac{27\sqrt3}{2}-\frac{81i}{2}.
-\]
-At the four-point exception it has a $5\times5$ minor equal to $512$.
-These calculations are certified by the accompanying script; alternatively
-the saturated ideals in Section~\ref{sec:small} establish reducedness there.
-Thus every complex point of $X_{G,q}$ has zero tangent space.
-
-For an affine scheme of finite type over $\C$, zero tangent space at every
-closed point implies dimension zero. In each local ring its maximal ideal
-satisfies $\mathfrak m/\mathfrak m^2=0$, hence is zero by Nakayama's lemma.
-The scheme is therefore a finite reduced complex scheme. It follows by
-base change and descent that $X_{G,q}$ is finite etale over $K$.
-This proves Theorem~\ref{thm:etale}. Notice that this argument uses the
-infinitesimal part of Ocneanu rigidity; separate use of its finiteness-of-
-categories conclusion is unnecessary.
-
-\begin{corollary}
-Under the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:etale}, the localized ideal defining
-$X_{G,q}$ is radical. For every local Artin
-$\C$-algebra $A$ with residue field $\C$, reduction induces a bijection
-$X_{G,q}(A)\to X_{G,q}(\C)$. In particular, there are no hidden nilpotent
-motions of a fixed solution of any order.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Set $V_A=\Hom_A(\rho,\rho\otimes\rho)=\bigoplus_hAT_h$ and
+$W_A=\Hom_A(\mathbf1,\rho\otimes\rho)=AS_0$.
+Choose isomorphisms $\ell_g:\alpha_g\otimes\rho\to\rho$ and define
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:distinguished-action}
+ D_g(t)=(\ell_g\otimes1)\mathfrak a_{\alpha_g,\rho,\rho}^{-1}
+                  (1\otimes t)\ell_g^{-1}.
+\end{equation}
+Rescaling $\ell_g$ by any unit leaves $D_g$ unchanged. In the Leavitt
+model take $\ell_g=1$; then $D_g(T_h)=\chi(g,h)^{-1}T_h$. Therefore
+\[
+ P_0=\frac1N\sum_gD_g,\qquad P_0(T_h)=\delta_{h,0}T_h,
+ \qquad\im P_0=AT_0.
+\]
+This proves the rank-one assertion over $A$, rather than merely after
+reduction. Choose a \emph{generator} $T_0$ of this free line, equivalently
+an element with nonzero reduction. A merely nonzero element such as
+$\eps T_0$ would not suffice. Any two generators differ by a unit.
+
+Choose also $v_g:\rho\to\rho\otimes\alpha_g$ and set
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:normalized-U}
+ B_g=(1\otimes\ell_g)\mathfrak a_{\rho,\alpha_g,\rho}(v_g\otimes1),
+ \qquad U_g=\eta_g^{-1}B_g,
+\end{equation}
+where $\eta_g\in A^\times$ is the scalar by which $B_g$ acts on $W_A$.
+It is a unit since $B_g$ is invertible. The spaces containing $\ell_g$
+and $v_g$ are free of rank one, so any changes multiply $B_g$ and $\eta_g$
+by the same unit. Thus $U_g$ is canonical: it acts identically on $W_A$.
+In the Leavitt model this recovers the displayed $U_g$, since $U_gS_0=S_0$.
+Now define $T_g=U_{-g}T_0$. These give a basis of $V_A$, determined up to
+one common unit. The dual basis $T_g'\in\Hom_A(\rho\otimes\rho,\rho)$
+is determined by $T_g'T_h=\delta_{g,h}1_\rho$; the perfectness of this
+pairing follows from the split fusion decomposition.
+
+Consequently the four factors in \eqref{eq:intrinsic-coordinate} change
+by $u^{-1},u^{-1},u,u$, so their product is unchanged. This cancellation
+would fail for unrelated basis changes $T_g\mapsto u_gT_g$: the factor
+would then be $u_g/u_0$. The normalized $U_g$ are what exclude that
+ambiguity.
+
+Let $F$ be an $A$-linear strong monoidal equivalence with tensorator
+$J_{X,Y}:F(X)\otimes F(Y)\to F(X\otimes Y)$, after identifying the simple
+labels in its source and target. Transport the maps by
+\[
+ \widetilde T_g=J_{\rho,\rho}^{-1}F(T_g),\qquad
+ \widetilde T_g'=F(T_g')J_{\rho,\rho}.
+\]
+Naturality and coherence transport \eqref{eq:distinguished-action} and
+its projector, so they transport the distinguished line. They also
+transport $B_g$ and, using the unit constraint of $F$, its action on
+$W_A$. The uniquely normalized $U_g$ therefore transport to the uniquely
+normalized operators in the target. The transported basis is consequently
+the target normalized basis up to its permitted common unit.
+
+The associator--tensorator identity is
+\[
+ F(\mathfrak a_{X,Y,Z})J_{X\otimes Y,Z}(J_{X,Y}\otimes1)
+ =J_{X,Y\otimes Z}(1\otimes J_{Y,Z})
+                  \mathfrak a_{F(X),F(Y),F(Z)}.
+\]
+Using it in \eqref{eq:intrinsic-coordinate} gives
+\[
+ F(\mathcal I_g1_\rho)=
+ \widetilde T_0'(\widetilde T_0'\otimes1)
+ \mathfrak a_{F(\rho),F(\rho),F(\rho)}^{-1}
+ (1\otimes\widetilde T_g)\widetilde T_0.
+\]
+Because $F$ is $A$-linear, the scalar on the left is still $\mathcal I_g$.
+Thus the equality of reconstructed scalars holds in $A$, not only modulo
+$\eps$. No strictness or preservation of a pivotal structure is required.
+
+Finally, in the strict endomorphism model $1_\rho\otimes T_g=\rho_A(T_g)$.
+In $T_0'\rho_A(T_g)T_0$, the first two sums of
+\eqref{eq:ring-rho-T} vanish by the mixed Leavitt relations. The right
+$T_0$ forces $k=0$ in the third sum, and the left $T_0'$ then forces
+$h=0$. This leaves $E_A(g)T_0/s$. A further multiplication by $T_0'$
+gives \eqref{eq:recon}, as an identity in $L_{2N}(A)$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{corollary}[Dual-number realization and reconstruction]
+\label{cor:dual-realization}
+Every dual-number solution at a nonexceptional complex point admits the
+normalized Leavitt, split fusion, and duality realization over $A$.
+Its scalar reconstruction is compatible with this base change and with
+label-preserving $A$-linear strong monoidal equivalences reducing to the
+identity. Thus the normalized realization hypothesis of the initial
+release is satisfied.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+Combine \eqref{eq:constant-sector}, Proposition~\ref{prop:ring-realization},
+and Lemmas~\ref{lem:flat-skeleton} and \ref{lem:intrinsic-reconstruction}.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{Vanishing of tangent spaces and descent}
+\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:etale}]
+At a nonexceptional point, Lemma~\ref{lem:flat-skeleton} gives a flat
+first-order deformation of the complex fusion category $\mathcal C$.
+For fusion categories in characteristic zero, the first-order associator
+deformation space is $H^3(\mathcal C)$, and it vanishes by
+\cite[Theorem~2.27 and Section~7.1]{ENO}. On the fixed split skeleton
+there is therefore an $A$-linear strong monoidal equivalence, reducing
+to the identity, from our deformation to $A\otimes_\C\mathcal C$.
+The simple labels are fixed, so no relabelling by $\Aut(G)$ occurs.
+Lemma~\ref{lem:intrinsic-reconstruction} now gives
+\[
+ E_A(g)/s=\mathcal I_g(\mathcal C_A)
+        =\mathcal I_g(A\otimes_\C\mathcal C)=E(g)/s.
+\]
+Thus $h(g)=0$ for every $g$, and $c_1=0$ by
+\eqref{eq:constant-sector}. Every nonexceptional tangent space is zero.
+
+The only exceptional points are those of Proposition~\ref{prop:alternative}.
+At the order-three point a maximal Jacobian minor of the raw residuals
+in variables $(E(0),E(1),E(2),C)$ is
+\[
+ -\frac{27\sqrt3}{2}-\frac{81i}{2}\ne0;
+\]
+its conjugate handles the conjugate metric. At the four-point exception
+a maximal minor is $512$. Section~\ref{sec:small} and the exact verifier
+supply these certificates. Hence every closed complex point has zero
+Zariski tangent space.
+
+At each such point the maximal ideal of its Noetherian local ring
+satisfies $\mathfrak m/\mathfrak m^2=0$, so Nakayama's lemma gives
+$\mathfrak m=0$. A finite-type affine complex scheme with this property
+is zero-dimensional and reduced, hence finite over $\C$. Faithfully flat
+descent from $\C$ to $K$, together with characteristic-zero separability,
+gives finite \'{e}taleness over $K$. This concerns the original localized
+ideal, not an ideal replaced by its radical.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{corollary}\label{cor:artin-lifting}
+For every local Artin $\C$-algebra $B$ with residue field $\C$, reduction
+induces a bijection
+\[
+ X_{G,q}(B)\longrightarrow X_{G,q}(\C).
+\]
+In particular, the raw solution scheme has no hidden nilpotent motions
+of any order.
 \end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+Apply formal \'{e}taleness to the nilpotent maximal ideal of $B$.
+\end{proof}
 
